Subject: NT 3.51 Mac File Server Performance Mystery

I admin a DEC AlphaServer 1000 4/266 running NT Server 3.51 (Service
Pack 4).  Among other things, it acts as an AppleShare file server and
seed router for our building.  Up until this month, its given us nothing
but incredible performance...better even than the AWGS 8150 with
AppleShare 4.2 we tested.  But a few weeks ago we had a freak power
outage.  When the power came back up and I rebooted the Alpha,
everything seemed just fine, except for one thing, the File Server for
Macintosh was significantly (perhaps 30% slower) less responsive than it
was before.

The netBIOS/netBEUI/SMB services seem unaffected, performance-wise.
Same goes with the WWW/FTP server.  The symptoms its exhibiting are:
greater latency in mounting the volume on the desktop of client
machines, greater latency in listing volume contents when the volume is
opened (regardless of 'view' listing style), and a very marked latency
in running apps over the network and copying files.  The number of
client macs mounting the server doesnt seem to be relevent to this
performance drop. Ive tried starting, stopping, and restarting the Mac
File Server service several times, as well as rebooting the server with
different options.  There doesnt seem to be any affect on the problem,
nor does there seem to be any way under NT of allocating more cache to a
particular service, which I thought might solve the problem.

Can this be attributed at all to Service Pack 4?  Has anyone else ever
seen a problem like this or have any ideas on what might be causing it,
or how on earth the power outage couldve affected it in this way?

Thanks in advance for any help you can provide me.

